From c307f7beb81e6d5c8ae82cb5e3bcc29222aaa795 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: malciller <46381560+malciller@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 22:30:39 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] feat(latency): measure feed network latency, not
 inter-arrival gaps

The ws_feed metric recorded the gap between consecutive messages, so a quiet
or bursty feed looked like latency and saturated the profiler ceiling (the
pinned 2.0s). Replace it with a clock-corrected one-way latency derived from
each message's server event timestamp:

- network_latency: per-venue clock-offset estimator (sliding-window min filter
  over local_recv - server_event, floored by half the min ping RTT), an
  RFC3339 parser, and record_feed_event_s/ms.
- Feed freshness gate: drop stale/replayed timestamps (closed-market quotes)
  instead of reporting the staleness as enormous latency, which otherwise
  decays toward the window length and pins the metric.
- Size network profilers for a latency metric: 1ms buckets, 60s range, so a
  multi-second stall is preserved rather than clamped.
- Extract real server timestamps per venue: Hyperliquid data.time, Alpaca t,
  Kraken book timestamp, Lighter top-level timestamp.
- Alpaca: stop recording SSE idle as a ws_ping sample; it is a staleness
  signal, not an RTT, and pinned the venue ping histogram at its 60s ceiling.
- Round span_of_seconds to nearest ns instead of truncating a bucket.
